\section*{Oracle-free computation of the limiting random 3-SAT threshold}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. The limiting random 3-SAT threshold is claimed computable to any requested accuracy by one finite deterministic procedure.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Deletion bounds certify rational lower brackets, and finite hierarchical soft-pressure trials certify upper brackets. A fair enumeration of both certificate families halts at every accuracy, using existence of the limiting threshold without needing an effective finite-size convergence rate.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The limiting random 3-SAT threshold is claimed computable to any requested accuracy by one finite deterministic procedure. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 235, the selected entries establish threshold existence, threshold computability, linear variance for $k\geq4$, and the earlier $O(n\log n)$ bound for $k=3$. The later linear three-clause variance improvement is not selected. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{FixedClauseThreshold.lean}, \texttt{SATComputability.lean}, \texttt{SATSharpness.lean}, \texttt{SATVariance.lean}. The inventory does not supply a named declaration for these Comparator entries. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

